<http://data.silknow.org/object/05fc9080-d1c1-327e-9151-1d34574aea47>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "Pair of sleevebands for a woman's robe, silk satin embroidered with coloured silks and gold thread, China, Qing dynasty, possibly 1850-1861."@en , "Pair of sleevebands. Embroidered sleevebands such as this one were ornamental haberdashery, made to be sewn onto the sleeves of Chinese womens' robes or dresses during the Qing Dynasty (1644-1911).  Commonly-used ornamentation vary from generic Chinese landscapes to scenes from Chinese tales, or motifs of birds and flowers."@en , "Pair of sleevebands for a woman's robe. Design of birds and flowers arranged in a rectangle, embroidered with coloured silks and gold thread in satin, stem and seed stitches on white silk satin."@en , "SLEEVE BANDS for Lady's Robe\nEmbroidered in silk on satin: satin, stem, Pekinese  stitch and  Pekin knot.\nCHINESE;  (Ch'ing dynasty);  mid 19th century;  (Hsien Feng).\nVuilleumier Collection. []"@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/6741b09a-9296-3229-ba5c-268a96e03c74> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/7ff013ae-0290-35a9-a379-40623ff498a8> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "SLEEVE BANDS for Lady's Robe\nEmbroidered in silk on satin: satin, stem, Pekinese  stitch and  Pekin knot.\nCHINESE;  (Ch'ing dynasty);  mid 19th century;  (Hsien Feng).\nVuilleumier Collection. []"@en , "Pair of sleevebands for a woman's robe. Design of birds and flowers arranged in a rectangle, embroidered with coloured silks and gold thread in satin, stem and seed stitches on white silk satin."@en , "Pair of sleevebands for a woman's robe, silk satin embroidered with coloured silks and gold thread, China, Qing dynasty, possibly 1850-1861."@en , "Pair of sleevebands. Embroidered sleevebands such as this one were ornamental haberdashery, made to be sewn onto the sleeves of Chinese womens' robes or dresses during the Qing Dynasty (1644-1911).  Commonly-used ornamentation vary from generic Chinese landscapes to scenes from Chinese tales, or motifs of birds and flowers."@en ;
        ecrm:P43_has_dimension         <http://data.silknow.org/object/05fc9080-d1c1-327e-9151-1d34574aea47/dimension/1> , <http://data.silknow.org/object/05fc9080-d1c1-327e-9151-1d34574aea47/dimension/2> ;
